Contract excerpt: UNIVERSITY DEFENCE RESEARCH CENTRE FOR SIGNAL PROCESSING
CONFIDENTIALITY AGREEMENT
DATE: 2011
PARTIES:
(1) …………………………………. registered office at …………………………………………………….. (hereafter “The Company”);
(2) ………………………………… address at………………………………………………………………… (hereafter "The University");
(3) THE SECRETARY OF STATE FOR DEFENCE, acting via the Defence Science and Technology Laboratory, Porton Down, Salisbury, Wiltshire SP4 0JQ, England ("Dstl").
(collectively referred to as "the Parties" or individually as "a Party").
BACKGROUND:
A. The University and Dstl collaborate within the UDRC, focusing on signal processing, and both hold proprietary technical knowledge.
B. The Company possesses critical technical and commercial insights in signal processing.
C. The Parties intend to share and review one another’s technical and commercial information to explore whether their capabilities can be combined for mutual advantage ("the Purpose").
D. Such exchange requires safeguarding valuable technical and commercial data.
AGREEMENT:
1. "Proprietary Information" encompasses any material, whether written, oral, visual, machine-readable, algorithmic, sample-based, or model-based, disclosed by a Party (the "Disclosing Party") to another (the "Receiving Party") under this contract. Information in written or documentary form must be clearly labeled as proprietary or commercially sensitive at disclosure (e.g., "Proprietary" or "Commercial-in-Confidence"). Oral, visual, or machine-readable disclosures (including samples/models) must be marked as proprietary when shared and confirmed in writing within thirty (30) days, with protections retroactively applying during this window. Proprietary Information also includes anything discerned through examination, testing, or analysis of provided hardware, software, or samples, even if marking requirements aren’t met.
2. Subject to Clauses 4 and 7, the Receiving Party agrees to:
(a) keep Proprietary Information confidential, not sharing it with third parties without written authorization from the Disclosing Party;
(b) only duplicate Proprietary Information as necessary for the Purpose;
(c) refrain from using Proprietary Information for anything beyond the Purpose unless the Disclosing Party grants prior written approval;
(d) limit access to Proprietary Information to employees or officers who require it for the Purpose.
3. Confidentiality and use restrictions do not apply to information that the Receiving Party can substantiate with dated records:
(a) was already public at the time of disclosure;
(b) becomes public subsequently, not due to breach;
(c) was already in the Receiving Party’s possession with rights to use/disclose before receipt;
(d) is legitimately received from a third party with rights to use/disclose;
(e) is independently developed by or for the Receiving Party without reference to the disclosed Proprietary Information;
(f) is required to be disclosed by court order, with prompt notice given to the Disclosing Party if allowed by law.
4. If Dstl or The University must disclose information solely to comply with the Freedom of Information Act 2000 or the Environmental Information Regulations 2004, it does not constitute breach, provided efforts are made to allow the Disclosing Party to comment on the disclosure. However, the Disclosing Party accepts that its input may not be decisive, and Dstl or The University will ultimately decide, restricting disclosure to only what’s legally necessary and notifying the Disclosing Party in advance if possible.
5. The Agreement does not:
(a) grant the Receiving Party rights or ownership in Proprietary Information beyond what is specified or subsequently agreed in writing;
(b) guarantee the accuracy or suitability of Proprietary Information;
(c) reduce statutory rights;
(d) imply further contractual arrangements will follow;
(e) override government security or export rules;
(f) obligate any Party to share specific Proprietary Information.
6. All Proprietary Information and any copies remain the property of the Disclosing Party and must be returned promptly upon written request.
7. The Agreement lasts two (2) years from the stated date, terminating unless renewed in writing or replaced by another contract. However, confidentiality and usage obligations extend for seven (7) years post-termination.
8. This document constitutes the full agreement regarding Proprietary Information exchange for the stated Purpose and cannot be amended except in writing, signed by authorized representatives.
9. No Party may assign or transfer interests or duties under this Agreement without written consent from the others.
10. Anyone not party to this Agreement has no rights under the Contracts (Rights of Third Parties) Act 1999 to enforce its terms, though other remedies may exist outside this Act.
11. The Agreement is governed by English law and Parties submit to the jurisdiction of English courts.
12. Parties must first pursue alternative dispute resolution (e.g., mediation or mini-trial) for any disputes. If unresolved after two months, disputes are settled by arbitration via the London Court of International Arbitration, in London and in English, with no appeal rights to the High Court under section 69 of the Arbitration Act 1996.
Each Party may seek injunctive relief or similar orders from a competent court in the event of real or threatened breach.
Signatures for The Company, The University, and The Secretary of State for Defence are required.


